From 3d5f8253c218d66d4171e56b7dd24a8db027ba4b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dgabehar <dgabehar@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 12:17:33 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] server: keep evicted prompt cache entries in the slot dir and
 restore them on a miss

The RAM prompt cache (--cache-ram) frees an entry it evicts, and skips an
entry larger than its whole limit. On models that cannot shift or partially
reuse their memory (hybrid/recurrent such as qwen35, SWA), the next request
for that conversation then re-processes the whole prompt.

Seen in production (Loki, 36h): gabesrv06 qwen38-27b (--parallel 1) and
gabesrv10 qwen38-flash (--parallel 2), both --cache-ram 8192, hold entries of
3.2-7.6 GiB each, so a second conversation evicts the first. The 27B had 6+
full re-prefills of 50K-120K tokens (8-47 minutes each); flash had 60
evictions in 424 requests, with re-prefills up to 170,527 tokens (13.7
minutes) right after "making room for prompt cache entry, removing oldest
entry".

With --slot-save-path set, an evicted entry (and an entry too large for the
cache) is now written to the slot dir as an ordinary slot save file,
prompt-cache-<16 hex>.bin. When a request misses the RAM cache, the server
scans the slot dir and restores the file whose token list shares the longest
prefix with the request.

- The file is the slot save format with no changes: ggsq header and
  LLAMA_STATE_SEQ_VERSION, serialized tokens, state, SCKP checkpoints, SDFT
  draft state. The RAM entry holds llama_state_seq_get_data_ext output, which
  is io_magic + seq_id (8 bytes) followed by the payload that
  llama_state_seq_save_file writes after its header, so the spill writes the
  header and tokens and then the entry without those 8 bytes. The slot-dir gc,
  the cross-node sync and kvrepl_nstream_convert.py need no change: the
  converter validates a spilled qwen35 file and converts it to 2 streams.
- The atomic temp+fsync+rename write of action=save and the restore of
  action=restore are factored into slot_file_save/slot_file_restore, and the
  spill and the lookup use them. A disk hit therefore restores the checkpoint
  appendix exactly as an explicit restore does, and the checkpoint-based reuse
  fix (patch 0002 / PR #26004) applies.
- Prompts under 4096 tokens are not spilled or looked up. The disk is looked
  up before the RAM cache loads anything, and a disk hit must beat both what
  the slot holds and the best RAM entry by 4096 tokens and keep a quarter of
  the file (server_prompt_cache::MIN_KEEP_RATIO, shared with the RAM cache).
  If the restore fails, the RAM cache load runs as if the disk had not been
  tried (the slot's state went to the RAM cache first), so a bad file never
  costs a request its RAM hit. A prompt that a slot file already contains
  whole is not written again. A file that fails to restore is skipped until
  its mtime changes and forgotten once it is gone. The file name is a hash of
  the token list; a name held by a file with other tokens is never
  overwritten, a -<n> suffix is used instead. One prompt cache pass scans the
  slot dir once for all its spills and its lookup. The lookup also matches
  the LiteLLM hook's session saves and files pulled from peers, since they
  use the same format.
- All three RAM eviction paths spill: the size limit in alloc() and update(),
  and the token limit (n_ctx by default) in update().
- Disk use stays under the slot-cache gc (age cap, size cap, free-space
  floor). Nothing else deletes these files.

The spill write (with fsync) and the lookup run synchronously on the server
loop. While a multi-GiB state is written or read, every slot stalls (live:
~170 MiB written in 0.16-0.25 s, so ~5 GiB in a few seconds; that measurement
is the ceiling, not a test of production disk). This is deliberate: a writer
thread would have to hold the evicted buffer beyond --cache-ram until the
write finished, and the pods' memory limits are sized to --cache-ram. Marked
as a ponytail ceiling in the code.

Tests: test_slot_save.py::test_prompt_cache_disk_restores_evicted_prompt
(tinygemma3 with a 64-token SWA window, which cannot partially reuse its
memory either; covers an evicted entry, an oversized entry, a draft-model
entry with the SDFT appendix, and a no-slot-dir control that re-processes
everything, plus a no-size-limit case where only the token limit evicts),
test_prompt_cache_disk_skips_short_prompts,
test_prompt_cache_disk_failed_restore_keeps_ram_hit (a truncated slot file
that beats the RAM entry is tried, fails, and the request still reuses the
RAM entry: cache_n 5201, was 0) and
test_prompt_cache_disk_name_taken_by_other_prompt (a spill name held by
another prompt's file is left alone, the spill goes to <name>-1.bin). The
last three fail without their fixes.

Live on a real hybrid model (Qwen3.5-0.8B Q4_0, CPU, 12 threads). Prompt A
is sent, then three other conversations evict it, then A is sent again:
- --parallel 1, --cache-ram 150 (one ~110 MiB entry fits), 4.8K-token A:
  without this patch, the second A re-processes 4817 tokens (11.5 s, wall
  11.7 s). With it: 4 tokens, cache_n 4813, wall 0.37 s, of which the 114 MiB
  restore takes 25 ms.
- --parallel 2, same flags: 4 tokens, cache_n 4813, wall 0.34 s.
- --cache-ram 1 (every entry larger than the cache), 9.6K-token A: cold
  38.1 s; second A 4 tokens, cache_n 9629, wall 0.66 s.
- Cross-node: a --parallel 1 spill converted to 2 streams with
  kvrepl_nstream_convert.py and dropped into the slot dir of a fresh
  --parallel 2 server is restored by the very first request: 4 tokens,
  cache_n 9629, wall 0.38 s.
Not measured: production-size states (3-8 GiB) on the fleet's disks and
Vulkan nodes, so the stall per spill there is an estimate.

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
